<p>A while ago I <a href="/blog/2026/03/30/creating-emacs-color-themes/">wrote</a>
that multi-variant themes have become the norm - pretty much every popular theme
these days is a family of a few dark variants and a light one. And every such
family ships its own command for moving between the variants:
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">modus-themes-toggle</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ef-themes-select</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tokyo-night-select</code>,
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">batppuccin-select</code> and so on.</p>

<p>I have to admit that I had completely missed that Emacs has a built-in command
for this since Emacs 29 - <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">theme-choose-variant</code>. I learned about its existence
only recently, when I tried it with my own
<a href="https://github.com/bbatsov/tokyo-night-emacs">Tokyo Night</a> theme and got this
in return:</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>Theme `tokyo-night' does not have any known variants
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Not cool, given that the theme has four variants! So, what’s going on here?</p>

<!--more-->

<h2 id="the-command">The command</h2>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-x theme-choose-variant</code> looks at the theme that’s currently enabled and
switches to another theme from the same family. If there’s only one other
variant it just switches to it, otherwise it asks you which one you want. The
command is also available as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">toggle-theme</code>, which is a much better name for the
two-variant case.</p>

<p>You don’t need to install anything to try it, as a few of the themes that ship
with Emacs come in pairs - <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tango</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tango-dark</code>, <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">leuven</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">leuven-dark</code>,
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tsdh-light</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tsdh-dark</code>:</p>

<div class="language-emacs-lisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">load-theme</span> <span class="ss">'leuven</span> <span class="no">t</span><span class="p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-x toggle-theme</code> will take you to <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">leuven-dark</code>, and another <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">M-x toggle-theme</code>
will bring you back to <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">leuven</code>. Pretty neat! The old theme gets disabled before
the new one is loaded, so you don’t end up with one theme stacked on top of the
other. (I wrote about that problem in
<a href="/blog/2025/02/03/clean-unloading-of-emacs-themes/">Clean Unloading of Emacs Themes</a>)</p>

<p>Things are a bit different with the Modus themes. There are eight of them in
Emacs these days, so with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">modus-operandi</code> enabled you won’t get a toggle, but a
prompt listing the other seven. And a theme that’s on its own, like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">wombat</code>,
gives you the same error I got:</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>Theme `wombat' does not have any known variants
</code></pre></div></div>

<h2 id="how-themes-opt-in">How themes opt in</h2>

<p>So, how does Emacs know that <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">leuven</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">leuven-dark</code> belong together? In case
you’re wondering - it doesn’t guess this from their names. In Emacs 29 <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">deftheme</code>
started to accept a property list after the docstring, and the theme has to say
what family it’s a part of:</p>

<div class="language-emacs-lisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">deftheme</span> <span class="nv">leuven-dark</span>
  <span class="s">"Face colors with a dark background."</span>
  <span class="ss">:background-mode</span> <span class="ss">'dark</span>
  <span class="ss">:family</span> <span class="ss">'leuven</span>
  <span class="ss">:kind</span> <span class="ss">'color-scheme</span><span class="p">)</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<ul>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">:family</code> is what ties the variants together. Themes with the same family are
variants of each other.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">:kind</code> should be <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">color-scheme</code> for a regular color theme. Themes that don’t
set it are treated as color schemes for the time being.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">:background-mode</code> is <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">light</code> or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">dark</code>. Nothing uses it yet, but the manual
asks theme authors to set it anyway.</li>
</ul>

<p><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">wombat</code> sets a kind and a background mode, but no family. My themes didn’t set
anything at all - their <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">deftheme</code> forms are just a name and a docstring.</p>

<h2 id="the-autoload-catch">The autoload catch</h2>

<p>Adding the properties to <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">deftheme</code> is not enough, though. They are stored on the
theme’s symbol when the theme file gets evaluated, and nobody has evaluated the
files of the variants you haven’t loaded yet. Emacs has to know the properties
of every variant before any of them is loaded.</p>

<p>The built-in themes solve this with a special cookie:</p>

<div class="language-emacs-lisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="c1">;;;###theme-autoload</span>
<span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">deftheme</span> <span class="nv">leuven-dark</span>
  <span class="o">...</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>When Emacs is built, the properties of every theme marked this way are
extracted into a file named <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">theme-loaddefs.el</code>, that’s loaded at startup:</p>

<div class="language-emacs-lisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">put</span> <span class="ss">'leuven-dark</span> <span class="ss">'theme-properties</span>
     <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">list</span> <span class="ss">:background-mode</span> <span class="ss">'dark</span> <span class="ss">:family</span> <span class="ss">'leuven</span> <span class="ss">:kind</span> <span class="ss">'color-scheme</span><span class="p">))</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>That cookie means nothing in a package you’ve installed from MELPA, though. A
third-party theme has to use the regular <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">;;;###autoload</code> cookie in front of its
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">deftheme</code>. Starting with Emacs 29 the autoload generator knows about <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">deftheme</code>
and extracts only the properties from it, so you end up with the same <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">put</code> form
in the package’s autoloads file.</p>

<p>Without this the command will find only the variants that you’ve already loaded
in the current Emacs session, which for most people means none of them:</p>

<div class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>`foo-dark' has no variants
</code></pre></div></div>

<h2 id="who-supports-this">Who supports this?</h2>

<p>I was curious how many themes have picked this up, so I went over the source of
60 or so popular ones. The short answer is “very few”. Protesilaos’s themes (Modus,
Ef, Standard, Doric) declare a family, and so do <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">doom-themes</code> and a handful of
smaller packages. Pretty much everyone else declares nothing, including big
multi-variant families like Solarized, Gruvbox and Kanagawa.</p>

<p>What surprised me more is that I didn’t find a single third-party theme that
autoloads its properties. As far as I can tell that leaves the built-in command
blind to the variants you haven’t loaded yet, and I guess that’s why the themes
that did the work kept their own commands anyway. (e.g. Modus loads its themes
without enabling them to learn their properties)</p>

<h2 id="a-few-rough-edges">A few rough edges</h2>

<p>The command is pretty basic and it has a few limitations you should be aware of:</p>

<ul>
  <li>It toggles only when the family has exactly two themes. Tokyo Night has four,
so I’d get a prompt every time. There’s no way to say “these two are the ones
I switch between”.</li>
  <li>It doesn’t know what’s light and what’s dark, because nothing reads
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">:background-mode</code>. You can’t ask it for “the light one”.</li>
  <li>It refuses to work if you have more than one theme enabled.</li>
  <li>It loads the new variant with a plain <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">load-theme</code>, so for a third-party theme
you haven’t marked as safe you’ll get the usual “Loading a theme can run Lisp
code” question.</li>
</ul>

<p>That last one is the reason <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">modus-themes-toggle</code> is still around. Protesilaos
considered dropping it in favor of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">toggle-theme</code>, and decided against it
because of the prompts.</p>

<p><strong>Note:</strong> This is a command you invoke by hand. If you want Emacs to follow the
appearance of your OS or the time of the day, check out
<a href="/blog/2026/03/29/automatic-light-dark-theme-switching/">Automatic Light/Dark Theme Switching</a>.</p>

<h2 id="what-about-older-emacs-versions">What about older Emacs versions?</h2>

<p>The good news is that the properties are harmless there. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">deftheme</code> has been
ignoring any extra arguments for ages (I checked all the way back to Emacs 24),
so a theme that declares a family will load just fine on Emacs 27 or 28.</p>

<p>The bad news is that <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">theme-choose-variant</code> doesn’t exist before Emacs 29, so
the users of the older versions get nothing out of the properties. The autoload trick doesn’t translate either - the old autoload
generator doesn’t know about <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">deftheme</code>, so it copies the whole form into the
autoloads file and the theme gets declared at startup.</p>

<p>Both Tokyo Night and
<a href="https://github.com/bbatsov/batppuccin-emacs">Batppuccin</a> support Emacs 27.1,
which means I can’t rely on the built-in command and I need one of my own
anyway. It’s not much code:</p>

<div class="language-emacs-lisp highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">defconst</span> <span class="nv">tokyo-night--variants</span>
  <span class="o">'</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">tokyo-night</span> <span class="nv">tokyo-night-storm</span> <span class="nv">tokyo-night-moon</span> <span class="nv">tokyo-night-day</span><span class="p">))</span>

<span class="c1">;;;###autoload</span>
<span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">defun</span> <span class="nv">tokyo-night-select</span> <span class="p">()</span>
  <span class="s">"Select and load a Tokyo theme variant interactively."</span>
  <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">interactive</span><span class="p">)</span>
  <span class="p">(</span><span class="k">let*</span> <span class="p">((</span><span class="nv">names</span> <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">mapcar</span> <span class="nf">#'</span><span class="nb">symbol-name</span> <span class="nv">tokyo-night--variants</span><span class="p">))</span>
         <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">choice</span> <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">intern</span> <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">completing-read</span> <span class="s">"Tokyo theme: "</span> <span class="nv">names</span> <span class="no">nil</span> <span class="no">t</span><span class="p">))))</span>
    <span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">mapc</span> <span class="nf">#'</span><span class="nv">disable-theme</span> <span class="nv">tokyo-night--variants</span><span class="p">)</span>
    <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">load-theme</span> <span class="nv">choice</span> <span class="no">t</span><span class="p">)</span>
    <span class="p">(</span><span class="k">setq</span> <span class="nv">tokyo-night--current</span> <span class="nv">choice</span><span class="p">)</span>
    <span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">run-hook-with-args</span> <span class="ss">'tokyo-night-after-load-hook</span> <span class="nv">choice</span><span class="p">)))</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The theme knows its own variants, so there are no properties to autoload and no
theme directories to scan. It works the same on every Emacs version, it doesn’t
ask you to confirm anything, and it can do things that only make sense for this
theme, like running a hook after the switch. That’s why I think that theme
authors who still support older Emacs versions shouldn’t rely on
<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">theme-choose-variant</code> for now. Just ship your own command, as most themes
already do.</p>

<p>Of course, nothing stops you from doing both. Declaring a family takes only a
few lines per variant, and it makes the theme play nice with the built-in
command on Emacs 29+. I’ll certainly add the properties to my themes, but their
own commands are here to stay.</p>

<p>Do you use <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">theme-choose-variant</code>? Did you even know it existed? I’d love to
hear about it in the comments!</p>

<p>That’s all I have for you today. Keep hacking!</p>